}}The [[Group GT2]] version of the Ford GT was built by Ohio-based Doran Enterprises and named Ford GT-R Mk. VII.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Kilbey |first=Stephen |date=2023-12-13 |title=GTE Through The Years: Ford |url=https://www.dailysportscar.com/2023/12/13/gte-through-the-years-ford.html |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231216173911/https://www.dailysportscar.com/2023/12/13/gte-through-the-years-ford.html |archive-date=2023-12-16 |access-date=2026-05-20 |website=Dailysportscar |language=en-GB}}</ref> The project began in 2006 as the brainchild of Oliver Kuttner and fully realized by Kevin Doran and his company, Doran Enterprise.<ref name=":7">{{Cite web |date=2006-12-05 |title=Kuttner Doran GT-R |url=https://www.fordgtforum.com/forums/threads/kuttner-doran-gt-r.3993/ |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260521003623/https://www.fordgtforum.com/forums/threads/kuttner-doran-gt-r.3993/page-2 |archive-date=2026-05-21 |access-date=2026-05-21 |website=The Ford GT Forum |language=en-US}}</ref><ref name=":6">{{Cite web |date=2026-05-22 |title=Robertson Racing 2008 Doran Ford GT-R GT2 Race Car |url=https://bringatrailer.com/listing/2008-ford-gt-lmgt2-race-car/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230214145418/https://bringatrailer.com/listing/2008-ford-gt-lmgt2-race-car/ |archive-date=2023-02-14 |access-date=2026-05-21 |website=[[Bring a Trailer]] |language=en-US}}</ref> Robertson Racing would place the first order in late 2007 (chassis #002). With their crew chief, Andrew "H" Smith, they would finish what was a mostly empty chassis before its first race at [[2008 12 Hours of Sebring|Sebring]].<ref name=":8">{{Cite web |last=Jacobs |first=Erik |date=2014-12-01 |title=The Ford GTs of Robertson Racing |url=https://motoiq.com/the-ford-gts-of-robertson-racing/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260213211231/https://motoiq.com/the-ford-gts-of-robertson-racing/ |archive-date=2026-02-13 |access-date=2026-05-21 |website=MotoIQ |language=en-US}}</ref> [[Tim Pappas|Black Swan Racing]] also placed an order for a GT-R Mk. VII.<ref name=":7" /> A total of three chassis were built.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Ford GT-R Mk.7 - Racing Sports Cars |url=https://www.racingsportscars.com/suffix/Ford/GT/-R%20Mk.7.html |access-date=2026-05-21 |website=www.racingsportscars.com}}</ref> 

It features a 5.0 liter [[Ford Modular engine#5.0 L and 5.3 L Cammer|Ford Cammer]] dry sump engine, built by [[Élan Motorsport Technologies|Élan]] and prepared by [[Roush-Yates Engines|Roush-Yates]], mated to a 6-speed EMCO sequential [[transaxle]].<ref>{{Cite web | date=2016-04-15 |title=2008 Doran Ford GT |url=https://www.supercars.net/blog/2008-doran-ford-gt/ |access-date=2026-05-20 |website=Supercars.net |language=en-US}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Purcell |first=Alexandra |date=2022-10-30 |title=2008 Ford GT-R GT2 Race Car Up For Auction |url=https://fordauthority.com/2022/10/2008-ford-gt-r-gt2-race-car-up-for-auction/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240721082303/https://fordauthority.com/2022/10/2008-ford-gt-r-gt2-race-car-up-for-auction/ |archive-date=2024-07-21 |access-date=2026-05-20 |website=fordauthority.com |language=en-US}}</ref> Other upgrades include a wider track, new suspension geometry, and a more aerodynamic body with fender flares and additional front and rear spoilers.<ref>{{Cite web |title=2008 Ford GT-R Pictures, Specifications, and Information |url=http://www.fast-autos.net/vehicles/Ford/2008/GT-R/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20251017011043/https://www.fast-autos.net/vehicles/Ford/2008/GT-R/ |archive-date=2025-10-17 |access-date=2026-05-21 |website=Fast-Autos.net}}</ref> A removable rear panel covering the engine was made out of carbon for quick access and reduced weight. Brakes have been upgraded to ventilated six- and four-piston [[Brembo#Motorsport|Brembo]] calipers at the front and rear.<ref name=":6" />

Robertson Racing entered the car's first race at the [[2008 12 Hours of Sebring]] with drivers, Andrea Robertson, David Robertson and [[David Murry]]. The debut race would end in a retirement after David Robertson crashed into the turn 13 barrier in the eighth hour.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Sebring 12 Hours 8-9 |url=https://archive2.dailysportscar.com/viewArticle.cfm@articleUID=B4C65229-1143-FDC9-356756CE7123BC1E.html |access-date=2026-05-21 |website=archive2.dailysportscar.com}}</ref> Poor results through the first four rounds of the [[2008 American Le Mans Series|American Le Mans Series season]], including another retirement at the [[2008 Utah Grand Prix|Utah Grand Prix]], after contact with an [[Audi R10 TDI]].<ref>{{Cite web |title=ALMS Salt Lake City - Race Report |url=https://archive2.dailysportscar.com/viewArticle.cfm@articleUID=0523047D-1143-FDC9-35EB2752A197DA6A.html |access-date=2026-05-21 |website=archive2.dailysportscar.com}}</ref> [[Tim Pappas|Black Swan Racing]] (chassis #003), backed by [[Falken Tire#Motorsports|Falken Tire]], would join Robertson Racing and made its debut at the following round at [[2008 Northeast Grand Prix|Lime Rock]] which would continue through to the end of the season.<ref>{{Cite web |title=American Le Mans Series Preview |url=https://archive2.dailysportscar.com/viewArticle.cfm@articleUID=08B11CF9-1143-FDC9-35FA66B9A5DF9C77.html |access-date=2026-05-21 |website=archive2.dailysportscar.com}}</ref><ref name=":9">{{Cite web |last=Goodwin |first=Graham |date=2015-01-12 |title=21st Century Ford GTs |url=https://www.dailysportscar.com/2015/01/12/21st-century-ford-gts.html |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150402234546/http://www.dailysportscar.com/2015/01/12/21st-century-ford-gts.html |archive-date=2015-04-02 |access-date=2026-05-21 |website=Dailysportscar |language=en-GB}}</ref> Both teams would not have much success with the car in its first season, Black Swan Racing taking its highest finish of the year of eighth at [[2008 Monterey Sports Car Championships|Laguna Seca]].<ref>{{Cite web |title=American Le Mans Series Laguna Seca 2008 - Race Results - Racing Sports Cars |url=https://www.racingsportscars.com/results/Laguna_Seca-2008-10-19.html |access-date=2026-05-21 |website=www.racingsportscars.com}}</ref>

The [[2009 American Le Mans Series]] season saw a solo effort by Robertson Racing where they finished as high as sixth in class at [[2009 Sports Car Challenge of St. Petersburg|St. Petersburg]].<ref>{{Cite web |title=Acura Sports Car Challenge of St. Petersburg |url=https://archive2.dailysportscar.com/viewArticle.cfm@articleUID=7C6FD641-1143-FDC9-35505D9941C8B4AC.html |access-date=2026-05-21 |website=archive2.dailysportscar.com}}</ref> The results were lackluster like the previous year, mostly finishing toward the rear of the GT2 field. However, they did manage to score points in nine of ten rounds of the season. Robertson Racing then fielded the car in the [[2009 1000 km of Okayama|1000 km of Okayama]] in the 2009 [[Asian Le Mans Series]].<ref name="New Entries">{{cite web|url=http://www.planetlemans.com/2009/09/01/already-23-entries-for-a-%c2%ab-grande-premiere-%c2%bb/|title=Already 23 entries for a " Grande Première! "|publisher=|author=|date=2009-09-01|accessdate=2009-09-01|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20120307171836/http://www.planetlemans.com/2009/09/01/already-23-entries-for-a-%c2%ab-grande-premiere-%c2%bb/|archivedate=2012-03-07|url-status=dead|website=Planetlemans.com}}</ref> They wound up finishing sixth in class with drivers, Andrea Robertson, David Robertson and [[David Murry]].<ref>{{Cite web |title=Okayama 1000 Kilometres 2009 - Race Results - Racing Sports Cars |url=https://www.racingsportscars.com/results/Okayama-2009-11-01.html |access-date=2026-05-21 |website=www.racingsportscars.com}}</ref>

In 2010, at a preseason testing event at Sebring, a crash resulted in chassis #002 sustaining severe damage to one of the rear chassis castings. Chassis #004 was built by Doran, cannibalizing any parts that survived form the crash chassis, for the start of the [[2010 American Le Mans Series]] season.<ref name=":8" /> Murry and the Robertsons campaigned the GT-R Mk. VII for a third straight year with mediocre results. At the [[2010 Grand Prix of Mosport|Grand Prix of Mosport]], Andrea Robertson made contact with [[Dirk Müller (racing driver)|Dirk Müller]]'s BMW at turn 9, retiring on the spot.<ref>{{Cite web |title=ALMS,  Mosport - Race Report |url=https://archive2.dailysportscar.com/viewArticle.cfm@articleUID=C8E2A284-1143-FDC9-3599754EFB67AF4B.html |access-date=2026-05-21 |website=archive2.dailysportscar.com}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=American Le Mans Series Mosport 2010 - Race Results - Racing Sports Cars |url=https://www.racingsportscars.com/results/Mosport-2010-08-29.html |access-date=2026-05-21 |website=www.racingsportscars.com}}</ref> The final race of the season at the [[2010 Petit Le Mans|Petit Le Mans]] saw the largest contingent of GT-R Mk. VII's with two entries from Robertson Racing (chassis #002 and #004) and one from ACS Express Racing, who had purchased chassis #003 from [[Tim Pappas|Black Swan Racing]].<ref name=":9" /> In the third practice session of the weekend, [[Brandon Davis (racing driver)|Brandon Davis]] aboard the #10 ACS Express Racing entry, had a major accident, which caused extensive damage to the chassis which ended up being a complete write off.<ref>{{Cite news |date=2010-10-11 |title=Event>> 2010 Petit Le Mans Reflections - Speedhunters |url=https://www.speedhunters.com/2010/10/event_gt_gt_2010_petit_le_mans_reflections/ |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170619194210/https://www.speedhunters.com/2010/10/event_gt_gt_2010_petit_le_mans_reflections/ |archive-date=2017-06-19 |access-date=2026-05-21 |work=Speedhunters |language=en-US}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=ALMS, Petit Le Mans - Friday Morning Notes |url=https://archive2.dailysportscar.com/viewArticle.cfm@articleUID=6843300E-1143-FDC9-35C987BA490F28D5.html |access-date=2026-05-21 |website=archive2.dailysportscar.com}}</ref> The #04 Robertson car with [[Rob Bell (racing driver)|Rob Bell]], [[Anthony Lazzaro (racing driver)|Anthony Lazzaro]] and [[David Murry]] finished eighth in GT while the #40 entry finished 12th with Andrea Robertson, David Robertson and [[Craig Stanton]] at the wheel.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Petit Le Mans 2010 - Race Results - Racing Sports Cars |url=https://www.racingsportscars.com/results/Road_Atlanta-2010-10-02.html |access-date=2026-05-21 |website=www.racingsportscars.com}}</ref>

The [[2011 American Le Mans Series|2011 season]] began at [[2011 12 Hours of Sebring|Sebring]] with two cars for Robertson Racing. #004 retired with starter motor issues while the #040 finished eighth in class.<ref>{{Cite web |title=ILMC/ ALMS, Twelve Hours Of Sebring - GTE Class Round Up |url=https://archive2.dailysportscar.com/viewArticle.cfm@articleUID=E2A48034-1143-FDC9-35FC15258CDDE55D.html |access-date=2026-05-21 |website=archive2.dailysportscar.com}}</ref> The team elected to skip the next race at [[2011 American Le Mans Series at Long Beach|Long Beach]] to focus on the [[2011 24 Hours of Le Mans|24 Hours of Le Mans]] test.<ref>{{Cite web |title=ALMS, Long Beach Grand Prix – Race Preview |url=https://archive2.dailysportscar.com/viewArticle.cfm@articleUID=560347A4-1143-FDC9-35EE8992C3E2EF0B.html |access-date=2026-05-21 |website=archive2.dailysportscar.com}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Wittemeier |first=Roman |date=9 February 2011 |title=Le Mans 2011: Das sind die 56 Starter |trans-title=Le Mans 2011: These are the 56 starters |url=https://www.motorsport-total.com/24-stunden-von-le-mans/news/le-mans-2011-das-sind-die-56-starter-11020901 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210914180114/https://www.motorsport-total.com/24-stunden-von-le-mans/news/le-mans-2011-das-sind-die-56-starter-11020901 |archive-date=14 September 2021 |access-date=21 September 2020 |publisher=motorsport-total.com |language=de}}</ref> At the test, they had some contact with other cars and a malfunctioning pit speed limiter but completed the required ten laps of the [[Circuit de la Sarthe|Circuit de La Sarthe]].<ref>{{Cite web |title=Le Mans Test Day - Team By Team - Part Four |url=https://archive2.dailysportscar.com/viewArticle.cfm@articleUID=9DC77C44-1143-FDC9-35276849902B2329.html |access-date=2026-05-21 |website=archive2.dailysportscar.com}}</ref> 

The drivers for the 24 Hours of Le Mans were [[David Murry]], Andrea Robertson and David Robertson. In qualifying, Murry sets a 4:08.208, lining the team up in 55th on the grid, beating out only the #64 [[Jetalliance Racing|Lotus Jetalliance]] entry.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Le Mans 24 Hours - Thursday Final Qualifying (And Combined Times) |url=https://archive2.dailysportscar.com/viewArticle.cfm@articleUID=76EED161-1143-FDC9-35AE95CB6475FD8A.html |access-date=2026-05-21 |website=archive2.dailysportscar.com}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=Le Mans 24 Hours, Thursday Qualifying 1 |url=https://archive2.dailysportscar.com/viewArticle.cfm@articleUID=75657FDF-1143-FDC9-351A9248D7E5BDF5.html |access-date=2026-05-21 |website=archive2.dailysportscar.com}}</ref> The car suffered a left rear puncture in the fifth hour but managed to make it back to the pits.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Le Mans 24 Hours, Hour 5 |url=https://archive2.dailysportscar.com/viewArticle.cfm@articleUID=7FB0A00A-1143-FDC9-35A5F40B1C401361.html |access-date=2026-05-21 |website=archive2.dailysportscar.com}}</ref> In the seventh hour they had issues with the [[paddle shift]] and had to disconnect and reinstall their sequential shift system.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Le Mans 24 Hours, Hour 7 |url=https://archive2.dailysportscar.com/viewArticle.cfm@articleUID=8015EAF1-1143-FDC9-350E866A75DD5F79.html |access-date=2026-05-21 |website=archive2.dailysportscar.com}}</ref> In the 23rd hour, the third place [[JMB Racing]] Ferrari had to twice enter the garage for clutch repairs, which allowed the Robertson GT-R Mk. VII to claw back the position from seven laps down to take the final podium place.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Le Mans 24 Hours, Hour 23 |url=https://archive2.dailysportscar.com/viewArticle.cfm@articleUID=83845F0D-1143-FDC9-35330C45DCDF0520.html |access-date=2026-05-21 |website=archive2.dailysportscar.com}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=The 79th Le Mans 24 Hours By The Numbers |url=https://archive2.dailysportscar.com/viewArticle.cfm@articleUID=8484645B-1143-FDC9-3584610F8F68A4A2.html |access-date=2026-05-21 |website=archive2.dailysportscar.com}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Jupp |first=Ethan |date=25 June 2016 |title=This Ford GT Is The Ultimate Motorsport Love Story |url=https://www.goodwood.com/grr/event-coverage/festival-of-speed/meet-the-ford-gt-that-grants-wishes/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260217122136/https://www.goodwood.com/grr/event-coverage/festival-of-speed/meet-the-ford-gt-that-grants-wishes/ |archive-date=2026-02-17 |access-date=2026-05-21 |website=goodwood.com |language=en-GB}}</ref>

Roberston Racing continued their dual car campaign and followed up the Le Mans podium with another third place finish at the [[2011 Northeast Grand Prix]] for the #04 of [[Anthony Lazzaro (racing driver)|Anthony Lazzaro]] and Murry.<ref>{{Cite web |title=ALMS, Lime Rock - Race Report |url=https://archive2.dailysportscar.com/viewArticle.cfm@articleUID=1ADAC78C-1143-FDC9-3522A6EAA2D48741.html |access-date=2026-05-22 |website=archive2.dailysportscar.com}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=American Le Mans Series Lime Rock 2011 - Race Results - Racing Sports Cars |url=https://www.racingsportscars.com/results/Lime_Rock-2011-07-09.html |access-date=2026-05-22 |website=www.racingsportscars.com}}</ref> The team completed the rest of the American Le Mans Series season, finishing seventh in the standings, before winding down their racing operations to focus on [[historic racing]], thus ending the GT-R Mk. VII's career.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Dagys |first=John |date=2016-06-06 |title=Le Mans Flashback: 2011, Robertson's Podium in Ford GT |url=https://sportscar365.com/lemans/lemans24/le-mans-flashback-2011-robertsons-podium-in-ford-gt/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250811112635/https://sportscar365.com/lemans/lemans24/le-mans-flashback-2011-robertsons-podium-in-ford-gt/ |archive-date=2025-08-11 |access-date=2026-05-22 |website=Sportscar365 |language=en-US}}</ref> 
{{Clear}}

====Ford GT GT3====
[[File:Blancpain Endurance Series - Ford GT - 003.jpg|left|thumb|Ford GT GT3]]

The Ford GT was also homologated for the [[Group GT3|FIA GT3]] rules by Matech Concepts. The Ford GT GT3 was involved in numerous championships including the [[FIA GT3 European Championship]], [[FIA GT1 World Championship]], [[Blancpain Endurance Series]], and others. The GT3 version is slower than the GT1 version (rated at around {{convert|500|hp|kW|0|abbr=on}} instead of {{convert|600|hp|kW|0|abbr=on}}) and features different bodywork.

